## Transforming Data Between Formats

Data transformation takes input in one shape and produces output in another. The most common transformations are: number to text, Map to Vec, and Vec to Map.

### Vec[Int] to Vec[Str]: Numbers to Words

```xiom
fn numbers_to_words(nums: &Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Str] {
  let result: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < nums.len() {
    let n = match nums.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    let word = "cool";
    if n >= 30 { word = "hot"; }
    else if n >= 20 { word = "warm"; }
    result.push(word);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let temps: Vec[Int] = Vec[Int].new();
  temps.push(15); temps.push(25); temps.push(35); temps.push(18);
  let words = numbers_to_words(&temps);
  io.println(string.int_to_str(words.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

### Map Entries to Vec

Converting a Map's key-value pairs into a flat Vec of strings:

```xiom
fn map_to_strings(scores: &Map[Str, Int], names: &Vec[Str]) -> Vec[Str] {
  let result: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < names.len() {
    let name = match names.get(i) { Some(n) => n, None => "" };
    let score = match scores.get(&name) { Some(s) => s, None => 0 };
    result.push(name + ": " + string.int_to_str(score));
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Vec to Map

```xiom
fn zip_to_map(keys: &Vec[Str], values: &Vec[Int]) -> Map[Str, Int] {
  let result: Map[Str, Int] = Map[Str, Int].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < keys.len() {
    let key = match keys.get(i) { Some(k) => k, None => "" };
    let val = match values.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    result.insert(key, val);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

These three patterns — number to text, Map to Vec, Vec to Map — cover the vast majority of data conversion needs in real systems.

